{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift (≈ -1 EUR in week 1, widening to ≈ -4 to -8 EUR by weeks 3–4).",
    "Overstates rebounds/mean reversion: after a selloff, the model predicts a stronger bounce back to low/mid-70s than occurs. Damp mean-reversion strength (shrink deviations from the latest price by ~30–50%).",
    "Lag in downtrends: when the recent 30d history is declining, the model turns up too early. Add a trend-following correction: if 10–20d slope is negative, tilt the whole path down (increasing with horizon).",
    "Overshoot after short-term spikes: when the last observed price jumps (e.g., into 71–73), the model extrapolates too much persistence. Cap post-spike persistence by pulling days 1–7 forecasts back toward the last close (e.g., subtract ~0.5–1.5 EUR if forecast > last close + 1).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors increase with forecast horizon (weeks 3–4 worst). Use horizon-scaled shrinkage toward a conservative anchor (e.g., last close or 10d average), increasing linearly with horizon (minimal in week 1, strong by week 4)."
  ]
}
